WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 5: The Mango Fever of 1968: When Mao's Fruit Became Sacred

1
00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:05.040
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:06.240
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:13.120
[Rebecca] Throughout human civilization, religious worship has centered upon divine texts, golden relics, and sacred mountains.

4
00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:26.160
[Rebecca] Yet in the summer of 1968, in the radical boiling cauldron of China's cultural revolution, an entire nation of 700 million people turned their collective adoration towards something altogether unexpected.

5
00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:29.120
[Rebecca] A sweet, tropical yellow fruit.

6
00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:37.920
[Rebecca] What began as an ordinary diplomatic gift of mangoes from the foreign minister of Pakistan was transformed overnight into an absolute national obsession.

7
00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:49.760
[Rebecca] For nearly two years, mangoes were preserved in formaldehyde, sealed in paraffin wax, enshrined under glass bell jars, and paraded through city streets like the relics of medieval saints.

8
00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:55.840
[Rebecca] To question, the divine holiness of a mango was to commit high treason punishable by death.

9
00:00:55.840 --> 00:01:02.000
[Rebecca] This is the bizarre, tragic, and unbelievable true story of the mango fever of 1968.

10
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:05.200
[George] Here is George with the story.

11
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:26.800
[George] Two years earlier, Chairman Mao Zedong had unleashed the great proletarian cultural revolution, urging millions of idealistic youth to form Red Guard brigades and smash traditional culture, bourgeois authority, and intellectual elites.

12
00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:33.440
[George] By 1968, however, the Red Guards had completely fractured into rival ideological factions.

13
00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:46.560
[George] At Xinghua University, China's premier polytechnic institution, two militant student factions, the Jingangshan and the April 14th Corps, turned the historic campus into a fortified war zone.

14
00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:56.240
[George] Students armed themselves with machine guns, home-brewed grenades, automatic rifles, and even heavy trench mortars built in university laboratories.

15
00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:06.040
[George] For 100 consecutive days, students sniped at each other from dormitory rooftops and university halls, killing 18 classmates and wounding over a thousand.

16
00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:09.080
[George] Beijing was spiraling into civil war.

17
00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:16.520
[George] Realizing that student violence was threatening the stability of his regime, Mao decided to bring the Red Guards to heel.

18
00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:32.600
[George] On July 27, 1968, Mao dispatched 30,000 unarmed factory workers from Beijing's state enterprises, organized as the worker peasant Mao Zedong thought propaganda teams to occupy the university and disarm the warring students.

19
00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:35.240
[George] The operation at Xinghua was brutal.

20
00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:46.360
[George] Enraged at the intrusion of common factory workers, the radical student Red Guards opened fire, killing five workers and injuring more than 700 with spears and gunfire.

21
00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:54.280
[George] Yet, the sheer volume of disciplined workers overwhelmed the campus, successfully disarming the students and restoring order.

22
00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:56.280
[George] Mao was deeply relieved.

23
00:02:56.280 --> 00:03:04.680
[George] For months, he had struggled with how to officially transfer political authority away from the chaotic students and into the hands of the working class.

24
00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:09.800
[George] A symbolic opportunity presented itself on August 4, 1968.

25
00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:17.000
[George] Mayan Arshed Hussein, the visiting foreign minister of Pakistan, met with Chairman Mao in the Great Hall of the People.

26
00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:25.720
[George] As a polite diplomatic gesture, the Pakistani minister presented Mao with a wooden crate containing roughly forty ripe, golden mangoes.

27
00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:35.480
[George] In 1960s northern China, mangoes were an exotic, mysterious tropical fruit that practically no ordinary citizen had ever seen or tasted.

28
00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:42.760
[George] Mao, who disliked sweet fruits and suffered from dental decay, looked at the crate of mangoes and made a fateful decision.

29
00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:56.080
[George] He instructed his chief of protocol to take the fruit and deliver it directly to the worker peasant propaganda team occupying Tsinghua University, declaring that the gift was an expression of his profound gratitude for their sacrifice.

30
00:03:56.080 --> 00:04:04.960
[George] When the delivery trucks arrived at the Tsinghua campus on the afternoon of August 5th, the workers were overcome with staggering, weeping hysteria.

31
00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:11.360
[George] In the fever-pitch climate of the Cultural Revolution, Mao was not viewed merely as a political head of state.

32
00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:17.040
[George] He was venerated as the Red Sun, an omniscient, godlike savior of the proletariat.

33
00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:26.400
[George] The fact that the chairman had given up his own personal food to bestow it upon humble factory workers was perceived as an act of miraculous, divine benevolence.

34
00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:32.000
[George] Workers fell to their knees in tears, chanting slogans and waving their little red books.

35
00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:41.760
[George] A massive official assembly was convened in the university gymnasium, where the golden fruit was placed upon a velvet-draped altar beneath a towering portrait of Mao.

36
00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:45.600
[George] Nobody dared to commit the sacrilege of cutting or eating the fruit.

37
00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:50.080
[George] Eating the mango was viewed as consuming the flesh of Chairman Mao's love.

38
00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:56.160
[George] Instead, committees were hastily formed to determine how to preserve the sacred objects for eternity.

39
00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:08.960
[George] The workers distributed the forty precious mangoes to the major industrial factories across Beijing, including the first machine tool plant, the Beijing Textile Factory, and the Capital Iron and Steel Works.

40
00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:15.840
[George] At the Beijing Textile Factory, the arrival of a single mango was treated like the state visit of an emperor.

41
00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:24.080
[George] Workers in clean white aprons carried the fruit on a red sedan chair, surrounded by beating bronze gongs and fluttering red banners.

42
00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:31.720
[George] However, within forty-eight hours under the sweltering August heat, the natural laws of biology began to assert themselves.

43
00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:37.000
[George] The delicate yellow skin of the mango began to turn brown, soft, and wrinkly.

44
00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:39.560
[George] Panic swept the factory leadership.

45
00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:48.360
[George] Desperate to prevent the sacred gift from decaying, the factory committee rushed the mango to the chemistry laboratories of Beijing Medical University.

46
00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:55.320
[George] There, scientists placed the fruit in a bath of formaldehyde and sealed it inside a hermetic glass bell jar.

47
00:05:55.320 --> 00:06:05.960
[George] Other factories hired master wax sculptors from the Beijing Handicrafts Academy to fabricate precise, lifelike replicas of the mango in solid paraffin wax.

48
00:06:05.960 --> 00:06:15.640
[George] These wax replicas were placed upon polished red lacquer pedestals, covered with protective bell jars, and installed in the center of factory auditoriums.

49
00:06:15.640 --> 00:06:25.800
[George] Every shift of workers was required to line up, bow three times before the glass-encased wax fruit, and sing revolutionary hymns before beginning their day's labor.

50
00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:31.800
[George] What followed over the autumn of 1968 was an explosion of commercialized state idolatry.

51
00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:48.040
[George] The Beijing Textile Factory retooled its industrial presses to mass produce wax mangoes by the tens of thousands, distributing them in elegant presentation cases lined with red silk to factories, military regiments, and communes across all 29 provinces.

52
00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:52.440
[George] The symbol of the mango became ubiquitous across Chinese society.

53
00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:58.360
[George] Textile mills printed cotton quilts and children's aprons adorned with smiling golden mangoes.

54
00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:08.280
[George] Factories produced porcelain enamel washbasins, thermoses, and tea mugs bearing pictures of mangoes under the slogan, Long live the Working Class.

55
00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:10.360
[George] Long live Chairman Mao.

56
00:07:10.360 --> 00:07:18.240
[George] State tobacco factories released new brands of mango cigarettes, marketed as possessing a subtly sweet revolutionary aroma.

57
00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:24.960
[George] Chemical plants produced mango scented hand soaps that workers kept in glass boxes without ever touching with water.

58
00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:46.000
[George] On October 1, 1968, during the massive National Day parade in Tiananmen Square, hundreds of thousands of marchers watched in awe as a colossal parade float rolled past Mao's viewing balcony, carrying a towering twenty-foot plastic replica of a golden mango surrounded by singing children dressed in fruit costumes.

59
00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:54.240
[George] In one Beijing factory, despite the best efforts of the preservationists, a single real mango began to rot beyond saving.

60
00:07:54.240 --> 00:08:00.560
[George] The factory leadership held a solemn emergency session to decide how to handle the decomposing holy object.

61
00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:07.680
[George] They decided that the fruit must not be thrown into the garbage, which would constitute counter-revolutionary blasphemy.

62
00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:20.400
[George] Instead, they peeled the blackening skin, placed the decaying mango flesh into a massive 60-gallon industrial cauldron of boiling water, and brewed what was officially proclaimed as sacred mango tea.

63
00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:26.480
[George] The factory's 5,000 workers formed a line that stretched for half a mile outside the cafeteria.

64
00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:33.840
[George] Each worker walked forward, held out a small tin cup, and received a single spoonful of the boiling mango water.

65
00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:42.000
[George] Workers drank the lukewarm broth with tears streaming down their cheeks, swearing eternal loyalty to Chairman Mao and the Communist Party.

66
00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:51.440
[George] When the pot was empty, the bare wooden seed of the mango was retrieved, photographed with great reverence, and buried in a ceremonial garden plot.

67
00:08:51.440 --> 00:08:57.600
[George] Yet, amidst the surreal hysteria, the mango fever carried a terrifying and deadly edge.

68
00:08:57.600 --> 00:09:09.960
[George] In the autumn of 1968, a touring delegation arrived in the small rural village of Fenchu in Sichuan province, carrying a treasured wax mango in a glass case to be venerated by local peasants.

69
00:09:09.960 --> 00:09:11.800
[George] Among the crowd was Dr.

70
00:09:12.120 --> 00:09:19.560
[George] Han Guangdi, a respected, 52-year-old village dentist who had practiced medicine in the region for thirty years.

71
00:09:19.560 --> 00:09:20.120
[George] Dr.

72
00:09:20.120 --> 00:09:24.120
[George] Han examined the strange yellow object resting on the silk cushion.

73
00:09:24.120 --> 00:09:31.720
[George] Having never seen a mango before, the dentist remarked casually to a neighbor, it doesn't look like anything special.

74
00:09:31.720 --> 00:09:36.600
[George] To tell you the truth, it looks just like an ordinary yellow sweet potato.

75
00:09:36.600 --> 00:09:42.280
[George] The dentist's innocent culinary observation was overheard by a local political commissar.

76
00:09:42.280 --> 00:09:44.600
[George] The village erupted in outrage.

77
00:09:44.600 --> 00:09:51.480
[George] Denounced as an active counter-revolutionary who had maliciously insulted a sacred gift from the great leader, Dr.

78
00:09:51.480 --> 00:09:54.440
[George] Han was immediately seized by local militia.

79
00:09:54.440 --> 00:10:06.520
[George] He was thrown into prison, subjected to brutal struggle sessions where he was beaten with belt buckles, and forced to parade through the streets wearing a dunce cap with a rotting sweet potato hung around his neck.

80
00:10:06.520 --> 00:10:10.120
[George] Within weeks, a military tribunal sentenced Dr.

81
00:10:10.440 --> 00:10:12.200
[George] Han Guangdi to death.

82
00:10:12.200 --> 00:10:16.600
[George] He was marched to the village execution grounds and shot in the back of the head.

83
00:10:16.600 --> 00:10:22.120
[George] His death was hailed in local newspapers as a glorious victory against bourgeois blasphemy.

84
00:10:22.120 --> 00:10:33.720
[George] By the end of 1969, as the political temperature of China shifted and military discipline replaced mass hysteria, the mango fever vanished almost as quickly as it had arrived.

85
00:10:33.720 --> 00:10:44.920
[George] Millions of wax mangoes were quietly hidden in attics or melted down into household candles, leaving behind a haunting, tragic monument to the terrifying heights of totalitarian fervor.

86
00:10:46.160 --> 00:10:56.240
[Rebecca] The mango fever of 1968 stands as one of modern history's most vivid reminders of how powerful charisma and political theater can become.

87
00:10:56.240 --> 00:11:08.560
[Rebecca] What began as a simple diplomatic gift from Pakistan was elevated into a national religion, demonstrating how societies can find sacred, unquestionable meaning in the most unexpected places.

88
00:11:08.560 --> 00:11:11.760
[Rebecca] Thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

89
00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:13.280
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

90
00:11:13.280 --> 00:11:18.160
[Rebecca] Join us next time as we unearth another forgotten chapter from the pages of history.

91
00:11:18.160 --> 00:11:20.720
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

